  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: Monsoonal moisture in the form of cloud \ncover and light rain has brought a temporary reprieve to eastern\nWA and north ID this morning with limited daytime heating. \nHowever, clouds have cleared out a bit faster than forecast, so \ntemperatures will still reach into the 80s today. Further west \nin central WA, clearing skies have led to rapidly warming \nconditions. Highs will peak in the 90s for places like \nWenatchee, Omak, and Moses Lake. Models have finally come into \nbetter agreement with the evolution of the troughing off the \ncoast. The upper low will meander towards central and southern \nOR tomorrow and Thursday, leaving the INW vulnerable to \nthunderstorms. The concern for drier thunderstorms has started \nto increase with the main trough staying offshore, allowing the \nboundary layer to be both warmer and drier. The PWAT anomalies \ntomorrow will be of the most concern for dry lightning around \n100-130% of normal. The greatest risk for lightning tomorrow \nwill be in the Cascades in closer proximity to the upper level \nlow and near the Canadian border (10-20% chance) in the \nafternoon hours. By Thursday, PWATs increase further to 130-180%\nof normal, decreasing the risk for dry thunderstorms, but the \ndynamics and thermodynamics look more impressive for a stronger \nthunderstorm risk. Profiles look fairly impressive for some hail\nproduction with the bulk of the instability concentrated above \nthe freezing level and moderately steep lapse rates in the \n700-500 mb layer around 7.5-8.5C/km both Wednesday and Thursday.\nShear profiles look more interesting Thursday for stronger \ncells with 30-45 kt of effective bulk shear and they are \nrelatively straight hodographs, supportive of hail growth. There\nwill be a 10-20% chance regionwide for thunderstorms. Deep \ninverted V soundings have me concerned for strong outflow gusts \nboth Wednesday and Thursday around 30-50 mph as well. By Friday,\nthe upper trough will migrate towards Vancouver Island, keeping\nthe main thunderstorm risk fairly limited to far northeast \nWashington and north ID (10-20% chance).\n \nSaturday through Tuesday: Forecast confidence increases for\ndrier weather and mild conditions returning over the weekend and\ninto early next week as the trough departs the region and\nridging reestablishes in the INW. Temperatures over the weekend\nwill be in the upper 80s to upper 90s. By Monday and Tuesday\nnext week, temperatures will further warm into the low 90s to\nlow 100s. /DB",
